      "body": "## Finding description \n\nWhen calculating a user's reward share, `TrustBonding.sol` retrieves the balances and computes the fraction:\n\n```solidity\n// function: _userEligibleRewardsForEpoch()\nuint256 userBalance = userBondedBalanceAtEpochEnd(account, epoch);\nuint256 totalBalance = totalBondedBalanceAtEpochEnd(epoch);\n\nreturn userBalance * _emissionsForEpoch(epoch) / totalBalance;\n\n```\n\nThe vulnerability exists because neither the numerator (`userBalance`) nor the denominator (`totalBalance`) is snapshotted or frozen when the epoch ends. Instead of locking in the final epoch state, both of these view functions continuously perform live historical binary searches against the underlying `VotingEscrow` contract.\n\n### Checkpoint Collision\n\nBecause `TrustBonding` relies on these live historical queries, it is fully exposed to how `VotingEscrow` timestamps its checkpoints.\n\nIf a user calls `deposit_for` in the exact block that an epoch ends, the underlying `VotingEscrow` contract writes a new checkpoint stamped with that exact epoch-end timestamp. When `TrustBonding` subsequently queries the historical supply and user balance for that epoch's end, it receives the new, post-deposit inflated balances rather than the correct pre-deposit balances.\n\n### Feasibility on Base L2\n\nTo execute this, an attacker only needs a block to land exactly on the epoch boundary. On Base L2, this is mathematically guaranteed without any miner manipulation:\n\n* The protocol's `START_TIMESTAMP` is `1_762_268_400` (an even number).\n* The `EPOCH_LENGTH` is `1_209_600` seconds (an even number).\n* Therefore, every single epoch boundary falls on an even Unix second.\n* Base L2 produces blocks every 2 seconds (always even timestamps).\n\nEvery epoch boundary is guaranteed to perfectly align with a Base block.\n\n### Mathematical Proof\n\nAssume total supply is $S$, epoch emissions are $E$, and 3 users hold equal shares ($S/3$).\nBefore the attack, each user's share is calculated as:\n\n$$\\frac{S/3}{S} \\times E = \\frac{E}{3}$$\n\nIn the epoch boundary block:\n\n1. Honest users claim early in the block. `TrustBonding` uses the correct $S$. They each receive $\\frac{E}{3}$.\n2. The attacker calls `deposit_for` by $\\delta$ tokens in the same block. The boundary timestamp checkpoint is updated.\n3. The attacker calls `claimRewards()`. `TrustBonding` now queries the historical supply and receives the inflated denominator ($S + \\delta$). The attacker's personal balance is also inflated to ($\\frac{S}{3} + \\delta$).\n4. The attacker's claim is calculated as:\n\n$$\\text{attacker\\_claim} = \\frac{S/3 + \\delta}{S + \\delta} \\times E$$\n\nBecause $\\frac{S/3 + \\delta}{S + \\delta} > \\frac{1}{3}$ for any $\\delta > 0$, the attacker takes a disproportionately larger share than they actually earned during the epoch.\n\nThe sum of the payouts distributed by `TrustBonding` becomes:\n\n$$\\text{Total Payout} = \\frac{E}{3} + \\frac{E}{3} + \\left( \\frac{S/3 + \\delta}{S + \\delta} \\times E \\right) > E$$\n\nThe fractions exceed 100%, mathematically breaking the hard cap of the epoch ($E$).\n\n### Impact (Insolvency and Direct Theft)\n\nBy inflating their own reward fraction mid-block, an attacker causes the sum of all distributed reward shares to exceed 100% of the epoch's emission cap. This directly drains the `SatelliteEmissionsController` reserves.\n\nConsequently, the protocol becomes insolvent before all legitimate users can claim their share. Honest users who claim later in the window will encounter an `InsufficientBalance` revert. Because the protocol enforces a strict one-epoch claim window, these late claimers suffer a permanent, 100% loss of their legitimately earned yield with no recovery path. The attacker effectively steals the yield directly from these honest participants.\n\n## Recommended Mitigation Steps\n\nThe root cause is that `_userEligibleRewardsForEpoch` queries both the user balance and total supply at *exactly* `epochTimestampEnd`, which is the same timestamp that boundary-block `VotingEscrow` checkpoints are written to. Because `_find_timestamp_epoch` returns the highest-indexed point with `ts <= query`, any same-block deposit retroactively inflates both the numerator (`userBalance`) and denominator (`totalBalance`).\n\nA snapshot-only fix (freezing only the denominator) leaves the numerator exposed: an attacker who deposits at the boundary can still claim an inflated numerator against a frozen denominator ($\\frac{S/3 + \\delta}{S} \\times E$). This still exceeds their fair $1/3$ share and still drains the controller.\n\n**The correct fix: query one second before the epoch ends.**\n\n`VotingEscrow`'s binary search uses a strict `ts <= query` condition. Querying at `epochTimestampEnd - 1` mathematically excludes every checkpoint written on the boundary block. This fixes both the numerator and denominator simultaneously with zero new state variables and zero extra gas overhead.\n\nModify `_userEligibleRewardsForEpoch` in `TrustBonding.sol`:\n\n```solidity\n    function _userEligibleRewardsForEpoch(address account, uint256 epoch) internal view returns (uint256) {\n        if (account == address(0)) revert TrustBonding_ZeroAddress();\n        if (epoch > currentEpoch()) revert TrustBonding_InvalidEpoch();\n\n        // Subtract 1 second to exclude any VotingEscrow checkpoints written\n        // in the same block as the epoch boundary (ts <= safeTimestamp is strict).\n        uint256 safeTimestamp = _epochTimestampEnd(epoch) - 1;\n\n        uint256 userBalance  = _balanceOf(account, safeTimestamp);\n        uint256 totalBalance = _totalSupply(safeTimestamp);\n\n        if (userBalance == 0 || totalBalance == 0) return 0;\n\n        return userBalance * _emissionsForEpoch(epoch) / totalBalance;\n    }\n\n```",
